{"subjectId":"java","id":"generics-wildcards","title":"Wildcards, PECS & Type Erasure","group":"Exceptions & Generics","order":40,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>List&lt;Integer&gt;</code> is <strong>not</strong> a <code>List&lt;Number&gt;</code>. Wildcards (<code>?</code>, <code>? extends</code>, <code>? super</code>) restore flexibility, PECS tells you which to use, and type erasure explains generics' odd limitations.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-exchange\"></i>Generics are invariant</h3>\n<p>Even though <code>Integer</code> is a <code>Number</code>, <code>List&lt;Integer&gt;</code> is not a subtype of <code>List&lt;Number&gt;</code>. If it were, you could add a <code>Double</code> to a list of integers through the <code>List&lt;Number&gt;</code> reference.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        List&lt;Integer&gt; ints = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();\n        List&lt;Number&gt; nums = ints;          // not allowed\n        nums.add(3.14);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Compiler error</figcaption><pre><code>Main.java:6: error: incompatible types: List&lt;Integer&gt; cannot be converted to List&lt;Number&gt;\n        List&lt;Number&gt; nums = ints;          // not allowed\n                            ^</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Arrays are <strong>covariant</strong> (<code>Integer[]</code> <em>is</em> an <code>Object[]</code>), so the same mistake compiles and only fails at runtime:</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Object[] objs = new Integer[2];      // compiles: arrays are covariant\n        objs[0] = 1;\n        try {\n            objs[1] = \"text\";                // runtime type check\n        } catch (ArrayStoreException e) {\n            System.out.println(\"ArrayStoreException: \" + e.getMessage());\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>ArrayStoreException: java.lang.String</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-asterisk\"></i>The three wildcards</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Wildcard</th><th>Accepts</th><th>Read as</th><th>Can add?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>List&lt;?&gt;</code></td><td>A list of <em>any</em> type</td><td><code>Object</code></td><td>Only <code>null</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>List&lt;? extends Number&gt;</code></td><td><code>List&lt;Number&gt;</code>, <code>List&lt;Integer&gt;</code>, <code>List&lt;Double&gt;</code>…</td><td><code>Number</code></td><td>No (except <code>null</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>List&lt;? super Integer&gt;</code></td><td><code>List&lt;Integer&gt;</code>, <code>List&lt;Number&gt;</code>, <code>List&lt;Object&gt;</code></td><td><code>Object</code></td><td><strong>Yes</strong>, <code>Integer</code>s</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static double sum(List&lt;? extends Number&gt; nums) {    // producer: we only read\n        double s = 0;\n        for (Number n : nums) s += n.doubleValue();\n        return s;\n    }\n\n    static void fill(List&lt;? super Integer&gt; sink, int n) { // consumer: we only write\n        for (int i = 1; i &lt;= n; i++) sink.add(i);\n    }\n\n    static int count(Collection&lt;?&gt; anything) { return anything.size(); }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(sum(List.of(1, 2, 3)));         // List&lt;Integer&gt;\n        System.out.println(sum(List.of(1.5, 2.5)));        // List&lt;Double&gt;\n\n        List&lt;Number&gt; numbers = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();\n        List&lt;Object&gt; objects = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();\n        fill(numbers, 3);\n        fill(objects, 2);\n        System.out.println(numbers + \" \" + objects);\n        System.out.println(count(Set.of(\"a\", \"b\")));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>6.0\n4.0\n[1, 2, 3] [1, 2]\n2</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-right-left\"></i>PECS: Producer Extends, Consumer Super</h3>\n<div class=\"formula\">If a parameter <strong>produces</strong> <code>T</code>s for you → <code>? extends T</code>. If it <strong>consumes</strong> <code>T</code>s → <code>? super T</code>. Both → plain <code>T</code>.</div>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    // src produces T (extends), dest consumes T (super) — same shape as Collections.copy\n    static &lt;T&gt; void copy(List&lt;? super T&gt; dest, List&lt;? extends T&gt; src) {\n        for (T item : src) dest.add(item);\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        List&lt;Integer&gt; ints = List.of(1, 2);\n        List&lt;Double&gt; doubles = List.of(3.5);\n        List&lt;Number&gt; all = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();\n        copy(all, ints);                      // T = Integer, Number is a super of it\n        copy(all, doubles);                   // T = Double\n        System.out.println(all);\n\n        List&lt;Object&gt; objs = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(List.of(\"x\"));\n        Main.&lt;Integer&gt;copy(objs, ints);\n        System.out.println(objs);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>[1, 2, 3.5]\n[x, 1, 2]</code></pre></figure>\n<p>JDK examples: <code>Collections.copy(List&lt;? super T&gt; dest, List&lt;? extends T&gt; src)</code>, <code>addAll(Collection&lt;? extends E&gt;)</code>, <code>sort(Comparator&lt;? super E&gt;)</code> — a <code>Comparator&lt;Object&gt;</code> can sort a list of strings.</p>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-eraser\"></i>Type erasure</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>Type erasure</strong> — generics exist only at compile time. The compiler checks types, inserts casts, then replaces each type parameter with its bound (<code>Object</code> if unbounded). At runtime, <code>List&lt;String&gt;</code> and <code>List&lt;Integer&gt;</code> are the same class, <code>ArrayList</code>.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        List&lt;String&gt; a = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();\n        List&lt;Integer&gt; b = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();\n        System.out.println(a.getClass() == b.getClass());\n        System.out.println(a.getClass().getName());\n        Object o = a;\n        System.out.println(o instanceof List&lt;?&gt;);      // OK: unbounded wildcard\n        // o instanceof List&lt;String&gt;                   // error: can't check a type argument at runtime\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>true\njava.util.ArrayList\ntrue</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>You can't…</th><th>Because at runtime <code>T</code> is erased</th><th>Workaround</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>new T()</code></td><td>The JVM doesn't know what <code>T</code> is</td><td>Pass a <code>Supplier&lt;T&gt;</code> or <code>Class&lt;T&gt;</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>new T[n]</code>, <code>new List&lt;String&gt;[n]</code></td><td>Arrays need their exact element type at runtime</td><td>Use a <code>List&lt;T&gt;</code>, or <code>(T[]) new Object[n]</code> with care</td></tr><tr><td><code>x instanceof List&lt;String&gt;</code></td><td>Type arguments aren't stored</td><td><code>instanceof List&lt;?&gt;</code></td></tr><tr><td>Overload <code>f(List&lt;String&gt;)</code> and <code>f(List&lt;Integer&gt;)</code></td><td>Both erase to <code>f(List)</code> — same signature</td><td>Use different method names</td></tr><tr><td><code>catch (T e)</code> / generic exception classes</td><td>Catch needs a runtime type</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Use primitives as type arguments</td><td>Erasure turns <code>T</code> into <code>Object</code></td><td>Boxed types, or specialised streams (<code>IntStream</code>)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\nimport java.util.function.Supplier;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static &lt;T&gt; List&lt;T&gt; nCopies(int n, Supplier&lt;T&gt; factory) {    // instead of new T()\n        List&lt;T&gt; out = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();\n        for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) out.add(factory.get());\n        return out;\n    }\n\n    static &lt;T&gt; T create(Class&lt;T&gt; type) throws ReflectiveOperationException {\n        return type.getDeclaredConstructor().newInstance();\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {\n        System.out.println(nCopies(3, StringBuilder::new).size());\n        System.out.println(create(ArrayList.class).isEmpty());\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>3\ntrue</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-bridge\"></i>Bridge methods and heap pollution</h3>\n<p>When a class implements <code>Comparable&lt;Money&gt;</code>, its <code>compareTo(Money)</code> erases differently from the interface's <code>compareTo(Object)</code>. The compiler generates a synthetic <strong>bridge method</strong> <code>compareTo(Object)</code> that casts and delegates, so polymorphism still works after erasure.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.lang.reflect.Method;\n\npublic class Main {\n    record Money(long cents) implements Comparable&lt;Money&gt; {\n        public int compareTo(Money o) { return Long.compare(cents, o.cents); }\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        java.util.List&lt;String&gt; found = new java.util.ArrayList&lt;&gt;();\n        for (Method m : Money.class.getDeclaredMethods())\n            if (m.getName().equals(\"compareTo\"))\n                found.add(\"compareTo(\" + m.getParameterTypes()[0].getSimpleName() + \")\"\n                          + (m.isBridge() ? \" &lt;- bridge\" : \"\"));\n        java.util.Collections.sort(found);\n        found.forEach(System.out::println);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>compareTo(Money)\ncompareTo(Object) &lt;- bridge</code></pre></figure>\n<p><strong>Heap pollution</strong> happens when a variable of a parameterised type refers to an object that isn't of that type — typically through raw types, unchecked casts or generic varargs (<code>T...</code> creates a <code>T[]</code>). The compiler warns (<code>unchecked</code>); <code>@SafeVarargs</code> marks a varargs method that doesn't misuse its array.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>An <code>unchecked</code> warning means the compiler can no longer guarantee type safety: a <code>ClassCastException</code> may appear later, far from the real bug, at a cast the compiler inserted. Don't suppress it unless you can prove the cast is safe.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is PECS?</summary><div><p><em>Producer Extends, Consumer Super</em>: use <code>? extends T</code> for a parameter you read <code>T</code>s from, <code>? super T</code> for one you write <code>T</code>s into. E.g. <code>copy(List&lt;? super T&gt; dest, List&lt;? extends T&gt; src)</code>.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why is List&lt;Integer&gt; not a subtype of List&lt;Number&gt;?</summary><div><p>Generics are invariant for type safety: if it were, you could write <code>List&lt;Number&gt; n = ints; n.add(2.5);</code> and corrupt a list of integers. Use <code>List&lt;? extends Number&gt;</code> when you only need to read.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is type erasure and what are its consequences?</summary><div><p>The compiler removes type arguments after checking them, replacing type variables with their bounds and inserting casts. So generic type info isn't available at runtime: no <code>new T()</code>, no generic arrays, no <code>instanceof List&lt;String&gt;</code>, and methods differing only by type argument clash.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>List&lt;?&gt; vs List&lt;Object&gt;?</summary><div><p><code>List&lt;Object&gt;</code> is a list you may add <em>any</em> object to, and only a <code>List&lt;Object&gt;</code> can be passed. <code>List&lt;?&gt;</code> accepts a list of <em>any</em> element type, but you can only read <code>Object</code>s from it and add nothing but <code>null</code>.</p></div></details></section>"}